\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Mean performance and mean hyperparameter CV as a function of negative sign percentage for the \textit  {C.\ elegans}-matched ER topology. The truncated IPC diagnostic is denoted in orange, GR in red, MC in blue, and KR in green. The same performance--invariance tradeoff appears without the biological topology, indicating that global sign balance can act as a primary control parameter within this matched random-topology setting. A total of 50 trials were performed for each fraction.}}{12}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:er_frac_curves}{{7}{12}{Mean performance and mean hyperparameter CV as a function of negative sign percentage for the \textit {C.\ elegans}-matched ER topology. The truncated IPC diagnostic is denoted in orange, GR in red, MC in blue, and KR in green. The same performance--invariance tradeoff appears without the biological topology, indicating that global sign balance can act as a primary control parameter within this matched random-topology setting. A total of 50 trials were performed for each fraction}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Mean performance and mean hyperparameter CV as a function of negative sign percentage for the predicted-polarity-only \textit  {C.\ elegans} contact-count magnitude pattern after removing connections with unknown or complex signs. The truncated IPC diagnostic is denoted in orange, GR in red, MC in blue, and KR in green. Reintroducing inhibitory signs again traces the performance--invariance tradeoff, now with contact-count magnitudes held in place. A total of 200 trials were performed for each fraction.}}{12}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
